{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems, abbreviated a_c: a domain-specific, carrier-neutral constitutional calculus for representing and relating governed symbolic systems. Its stable concept identity is urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c, owned by this standard in urn:stdo:bounded-context:axiomatic-calculus. The concept identity does not identify an immutable edition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1,
          "quote": "# The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems",
          "start_line": 1
        },
        {
          "end_line": 43,
          "quote": "urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FPosition",
          "start_line": 5
        }
      ],
      "key": "axiomatic_calculus",
      "label": "Subject-defined a_c calculus"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Sigma comprises RecordKind, Sort, RelationKind, ConstraintKind, ResidualKind, FunctorKind, JudgmentKind and StopKind, each a finite closed identity set for one model family. The eight fixed RecordKind_ac identities have prefix urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind: and suffixes semantic-object, typed-relation, constraint, latitude, residual, traversal, transformation and judgment.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 193,
          "quote": "Every signature member is a finite, closed identity set for one model family.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 147
        }
      ],
      "key": "calculus_signature",
      "label": "Closed signature Sigma"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An abstract model M_b = (b,I,O,E,C,L,X,V,T,J) binds exact basis b and closed identity universe I. Population_M maps the eight core record kinds respectively to finite populations of semantic objects O, directed typed relations E, constitutional constraints C, latitude L, residual uncertainty X, traversals V, transformations T and judgments J. Local_M is their disjoint union; I = Local_M disjoint_union External_M.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 236,
          "quote": "M_b = (b, I, O, E, C, L, X, V, T, J)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 198
        }
      ],
      "key": "symbolic_model",
      "label": "Exact-basis symbolic model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "SemanticObject has exactly id, sort, context, owner, scope, basis and value. id, sort, context, owner and basis are identities; scope is the closed governed domain; value is sort-defined carrier-neutral content or a reference to exact content.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 291,
          "quote": "Every semantic object has exactly:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FSemantic%20Object",
          "start_line": 275
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_object",
      "label": "SemanticObject"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "TypedRelation has exactly id, kind, source, target, context, owner, scope, basis and qualifiers. Its endpoints resolve to members of I. qualifiers is a closed kind-specific record containing the direction, cardinality, preservation, loss, inverse, invalidation or other semantics required by the signature.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 313,
          "quote": "Every relation has exactly:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FTyped%20Relation",
          "start_line": 295
        }
      ],
      "key": "typed_relation",
      "label": "TypedRelation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Constraint has exactly id, kind, applies_to, predicate, context, owner, scope, basis, judgment_kind, latitude_ref and refusal. kind resolves one declared ConstraintKind and judgment_kind one declared JudgmentKind. The signature owns the predicate language and result assessment; a predicate not deterministically decidable declares the required probabilistic or human judgment kind.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 339,
          "quote": "Every constraint has exactly:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FConstraint",
          "start_line": 317
        }
      ],
      "key": "constraint_record",
      "label": "Constraint record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Latitude is an explicit permission set within a constraint boundary, with fields id, applies_to, allowed_variation, forbidden_variation, context, owner, scope, basis and invalidation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 356,
          "quote": "Latitude is an explicit permission set inside a constraint boundary:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FLatitude",
          "start_line": 343
        }
      ],
      "key": "latitude_record",
      "label": "Latitude record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Residual uncertainty is first-class model content. Residual has fields id, subject, kind, uncertainty, consequence, context, owner, scope, basis, re_entry and invalidation. kind resolves one declared ResidualKind; the signature closes its lawful subject, uncertainty, consequence, re-entry and invalidation domains.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 388,
          "quote": "Residual uncertainty is first-class model content:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FResidual",
          "start_line": 364
        }
      ],
      "key": "residual_record",
      "label": "Residual record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Material closure is closure_R(M,Z) = lfp(lambda Q: Z union required_R(M,Q)). It is the least lawful record closure under the exact dependency family R, rather than a minimum of prose, bytes, tokens, cost or cognitive effort.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 531,
          "quote": "closure_R(M, Z) = lfp(lambda Q: Z union required_R(M, Q))",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-010%20Material%20Closure",
          "start_line": 520
        }
      ],
      "key": "material_closure",
      "label": "Material closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Projection selects an exact view without changing selected meaning: project(M,Z,R) -> (M_Z,Boundary_Z) | gap | refusal. M_Z contains material closure; Boundary_Z records excluded, external, unresolved, translated and invalidation-sensitive seams for the declared consumer task.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 546,
          "quote": "project(M, Z, R) -> (M_Z, Boundary_Z) | gap | refusal",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-011%20Projection",
          "start_line": 535
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_projection",
      "label": "Model projection and boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared populations of a basis model without mutating that basis. Override, disambiguation, equivalence, specialization and translation exist only as typed relations with exact authority, direction, scope, basis, preservation, loss, refusal and invalidation semantics.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 554,
          "quote": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared `Population_M`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-012%20Overlay",
          "start_line": 550
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_overlay",
      "label": "Typed overlay"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A transformation t is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal v. Its fields are id, traversal, domain_model, codomain_model, context, owner, scope, basis, operation_authority, preconditions, preservation_relation, preserved, introduced, removed, external_preserved, external_introduced, external_removed, external_resolution_witnesses, residuals, evidence, provenance, stop_states, invalidation and re_entry. Application T[t](M_b,inputs) returns a proposed successor M_b_prime* or hold, gap or refusal.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 587,
          "quote": "A transformation is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-013%20Transformation",
          "start_line": 558
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_transformation",
      "label": "Transformation contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Judgment points to an unchanged subject and has fields id, kind, subject, subject_digest, context, owner, scope, basis, evaluator, authority, predicate, verdict, decision, performance, effects, evidence, residuals, provenance and decided_at. Foundations owns result semantics; decision preserves the original calculus/domain outcome and is not a second verdict enum.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 773,
          "quote": "A judgment points to an unchanged subject:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-014%20Judgment%20Separation",
          "start_line": 730
        }
      ],
      "key": "judgment_contract",
      "label": "Subject-defined judgment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A traversal v is a typed contract with id, domain, codomain, context, owner, scope, basis, preconditions, postconditions, authority, evidence, provenance and stop_states. F_K[v](X_v) -> Y_v | Omega_v binds one kind, exact traversal identity, typed input/result domains and closed stop refinement. The three kind identities are urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-d, :f-p and :f-h: deterministic evaluation/proof returning judgment without constructing its evaluated domain; bounded probabilistic interpretation/construction/proposal; and explicitly authorized human adjudication, respectively.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 851,
          "quote": "A traversal is a typed contract:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTraversals%20And%20Functor%20Kinds",
          "start_line": 800
        }
      ],
      "key": "traversal_contract",
      "label": "Traversal and functor-kind classification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis,signature,interpretation_contract) -> M_subject* | hold | gap | refusal. The star marks a proposed model lacking applicable semantic acceptance. The contract declares exact calculus basis; exact subject identity, inventory and bytes/semantic addresses; signature and compatible extension; mappings to every applicable record population; interpreter identity, capability, provenance and stops; structural evaluation; semantic selection and acceptance authority; source re-entry and invalidation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 933,
          "quote": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FInterpretation%20And%20Models",
          "start_line": 912
        }
      ],
      "key": "subject_interpretation",
      "label": "Subject interpretation and proposed model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The distinct layers are a_c, pure calculus; a_c.X, subject X interpreted as a model; and a_c.X.C, an accepted model encoded in carrier C. J_X is external semantic acceptance over unchanged M_X. D_C is deterministic carrier-admission judgment over unchanged G_C bytes using Profile_C and CarrierBasis_C. Admitted encoding evidence is the exact triple (G_C,J_X,D_C).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1058,
          "quote": "a_c.X.C   = that accepted model encoded in carrier C",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FSubject%20And%20Carrier%20Boundaries",
          "start_line": 1035
        }
      ],
      "key": "encoded_carrier",
      "label": "Encoded carrier and external acceptance evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis is an external record issued after publication bytes exist: kind stdo.axiomatic-calculus-basis, schema_version 1, fixed a_c concept_identity, derivation_basis with immutable absolute predecessor release_uri, manifest_sha256 and nonempty absolute semantic-address principle_refs, and publication_basis with distinct immutable successor release_uri, manifest_sha256, member_uri and member_sha256. Digests use sha256: followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1099,
          "quote": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis = {",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FExact%20Calculus%20Identity",
          "start_line": 1078
        }
      ],
      "key": "exact_calculus_basis",
      "label": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Design Module Method owns generic design-contract law and an adoptable realization profile. Its realization discipline favors low coupling, modular composition, immutable typed carriers, total or nearly-total semantic transforms and explicit effect boundaries, aiming to reduce ambiguity, entropy, avoidable debt, hidden authority/mutation and semantic drift while increasing refactorability and reconstructable evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1,
          "quote": "# Design Module Method",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method",
          "start_line": 1
        },
        {
          "end_line": 75,
          "quote": "This method is therefore about realization design and implementation shape.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F1.%20Purpose",
          "start_line": 51
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_module_method",
      "label": "Design Module Method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The corresponding design profile is adopted at the boundary."],
      "definition": "Ontology is the accepted semantic-design authority subordinate to constitutional WHAT, derived from intent, Product, requirements and applicable graph/domain method. It comprises entities, identities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, atomic functions, higher-order composition, effects and projections. Accepted design separately owns structural HOW: topology, interfaces, carrier placement, modules, algorithms and local realization.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 406,
          "quote": "The Ontology is the prior **semantic-design authority** for the boundary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule",
          "start_line": 396
        },
        {
          "end_line": 517,
          "quote": "A class inventory, schema list, endpoint list, command list, or carrier census",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FProportional%20Design%20Sequencing",
          "start_line": 500
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_ontology",
      "label": "Accepted design Ontology"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["One governed design boundary B."],
      "definition": "M(B) is the complete material-relation network: entities, identities, relationships, cardinalities, invariants, authorities and transitions, functions and higher-order composition laws, topology, lifecycle states/transitions, public contracts, externally observable Product meaning, declared domains/admission conditions, failure/refusal routes, effect/closure laws, projections, module mappings and material algorithmic obligations. U(B) contains unresolved members. Decision completeness requires U(B) empty, joint satisfiability and semantic equivalence of every admitted network under governing Product, requirements and accepted design.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 549,
          "quote": "For one governed boundary `B`, the complete material-relation set is:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FDecision-Complete%20Symbolic%20Design",
          "start_line": 521
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_boundary_network",
      "label": "M(B), U(B) and decision completeness"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Foundation selection compares bounded candidate compositions supporting irreducible semantic or authority-bearing relations and generic mechanics. Its result may be a lawful selected composition, an explicit undominated frontier or a design gap. Native constructability alone does not establish the best realization.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 642,
          "quote": "Native constructability proves that the selected substrate can realize the",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FCapability%20And%20Foundation%20Selection%20%28%60STDO-UP-023%60%29",
          "start_line": 629
        }
      ],
      "key": "foundation_selection",
      "label": "Capability and foundation selection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The entity logical-completeness matrix identifies Entity, Identity, Authority owner, Declare/create, Read/project, Update/transition and Delete/retire. It applies to identity-bearing entities and independently governed value families. Entity lifecycle describes domain states and transformations and differs from operational module lifecycle covering build, release, deploy, observe and retire.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 775,
          "quote": "| Entity | Identity | Authority owner | Declare/create | Read/project | Update/transition | Delete/retire |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FEntity%20And%20Lifecycle%20Completeness",
          "start_line": 743
        }
      ],
      "key": "entity_lifecycle_evidence",
      "label": "Entity lifecycle evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authority is part of Ontology. Its matrix distinguishes Function/transition, Proposer, Evaluator, Verifier, Admitter, Executor, Projector and Retirement owner, rather than adding authority after selecting an operation's shape.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 786,
          "quote": "| Function or transition | Proposer | Evaluator | Verifier | Admitter | Executor | Projector | Retirement owner |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FAuthority%20Model",
          "start_line": 779
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_authority_model",
      "label": "Design authority model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The transformation-admission relation decides which candidate transformation becomes durable entity truth. It binds entity identity, predecessor basis, authority scope, candidate family, decision cardinality, admission predicate, durable result and supersession law. An authority matrix identifying who admits does not by itself determine this relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 827,
          "quote": "The authority matrix names who admits. It does not by itself establish the",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FTransformation%20Admission%20Completeness%20%28%60STDO-UP-016%60%29",
          "start_line": 815
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_admission_relation",
      "label": "Durable transformation-admission relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Atomic function families are the smallest parameterized families making entity lifecycles logically complete. Function-derivation evidence relates discovered functionality, entity, atomic function/template, higher-order composition, effect class, required authority and disposition. Higher-order categories may include unit/lift, sequential, parallel/applicative, fold/evaluation, retry/recovery, recursion/fixed point and projection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 943,
          "quote": "The Ontology must derive the smallest parameterized atomic function families",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FAtomic%20Functions%20And%20Higher-Order%20Composition",
          "start_line": 918
        }
      ],
      "key": "atomic_function_family",
      "label": "Atomic functions and higher-order algebra"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A Prime semantic atom is an irreducible design relation—atomic function family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle, topology, effect, closure or composition law—that cannot honestly be composed from other accepted atoms preserving meaning. Prime is determined at semantic-design altitude; realization size, naming, typing or independent testability does not establish it.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 969,
