      ],
      "key": "prime_atom_domain_law",
      "statement": "One atom may map to several realization units and one unit to several atoms only with explicit mapping and no rival meaning/authority. Atomic functions are not proven Prime before whole-family contraction. Every accepted atom declares its admitted domain and governs every admitted instance; later uses compose/parameterize it without local fork/shadow/specialization/reinterpretation. In-domain counterexample falsifies shared atom and requires re-entry at its design boundary, not feature replacement. Without declared domain an atom remains candidate and supplies neither Prime acceptance nor realization conformance.",
