      "exceptions": ["Intentional omission/flattening declares accepted loss and a failure condition.", "Implementation branches/test expectations need not be individually enumerated in Ontology."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1044,
          "quote": "Target\ndesign may add structural HOW, but cannot originate rival semantic meaning.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FOntology%20Projection%20Law",
          "start_line": 1029
        }
      ],
      "key": "ontology_projection_requirements",
      "statement": "Faithful projection uses accepted Ontology as semantic source and preserves its identity/relationship/lifecycle/authority/function/effect distinctions. Targets include domain/class/sequence/state views, authority/effect matrices, IACS, APIs/registers/SDK/CLI, schemas/codecs/manifests/catalogs, architecture/modules and implementation/test/proof inventories through accepted design and requirements. Binding names source and preserved laws. Structural HOW cannot introduce rival meaning. Public operations/messages/transitions/carriers/effects untraceable through accepted design to Ontology cannot enter active line. Code branches/tests trace to requirements plus design and preserve Ontology.",
      "uncertainty": ""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["prime_semantic_atom", "architectural_carrier_set"],
      "conditions": ["Prime policy applies to proposed top-level functions, classes, carrier types, schema records and modules."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1117,
          "quote": "Typed closure by itself is **not** sufficient reason to create a new top-level",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F5.%20Prime%20Law",
          "start_line": 1050
        }
      ],
      "key": "prime_realization_law",
      "statement": "Derive Prime atoms from logically complete candidate Ontology before top-level units; whole-family Prime evidence supports Ontology acceptance and downstream design/IACS/modules/functions/schemas/tests project accepted atoms. Typed closure alone does not justify new top-level types. Units are Prime-conformant only if projecting accepted atoms without new/duplicate/reinterpreted authority; topology/effect boundaries first require accepted Prime derivation. Size and one-to-one atom mapping are immaterial. Typical lawful reasons include admitted graph topology, typed carrier transform, effect, validation/admission or projection boundaries, subject to those prerequisites. Units solely shortening calls, aggregating unrelated convenience behavior, renaming old interfaces, adding wrappers, concealing mutation/fallback/orchestration, naming nonauthoritative payload variants, publishing internal detail without irreducible boundary, mirroring carriers with small changes or proliferating types for typing discomfort are not conformant. Prefer composition, existing lawful functions and fewer sharper boundaries/families; purpose is preventing semantic fragmentation/helper sprawl, not minimizing lines.",
      "uncertainty": ""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["design_boundary_network", "prime_semantic_atom"],
      "conditions": ["During Prime review and decomposition."],
      "exceptions": ["Many-to-one and one-to-many relations among boundaries are permitted; no artifact/review per concern is required."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1129,
          "quote": "Review cuts, realization units, runtime modules, and work items are distinct",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F5.%20Prime%20Law%2FBoundary%20And%20Governance%20Cost%20%28%60STDO-UP-005%60%29",
          "start_line": 1121
        }
      ],
      "key": "boundary_governance_cost",
      "statement": "Review cuts, realization units, runtime modules and work items are distinct and cannot be forced one-to-one for administrative convenience. Contraction accounts for governance cost as well as unit count and is unsuccessful if it increases authority centers, maintained truth surfaces, acceptance boundaries, dependency cycles, manual projection reconciliation or review burden. Evidence is proportional to semantic change and risk.",
